00:05:38.963 --> 00:05:43.023
When I first met Caroline,
she was one of my CAT students.

00:05:43.023 --> 00:05:47.287
CAT is the critical analytical thinking
course here at the GSB that all

00:05:47.287 --> 00:05:50.990
first-year students
are now required to take.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.489
So she was one of 16 students about
the group that were gathered here who

00:05:55.489 --> 00:05:59.419
was in one of my seminars in critical and
analytical thinking.

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:05.596
And the way I first got to know Caroline
is she cited Stan Delaney in her paper and

00:06:05.596 --> 00:06:09.433
said personal conversation
with Stan Delaney,

00:06:09.433 --> 00:06:15.010
Morgan Stanley Vice President and
his GSP graduating year.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.040
Well, I know Stan and

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.160
I was like, why is she citing somebody
that I know in her CAT paper?

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:25.595
She's probably figuring out
a way to get on my good side.

00:06:25.595 --> 00:06:31.420
The first time I met her sister Catha was
in the upper r buckle on the other campus.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.770
And as you know,
people run quickly through

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.610
upper r buckle because it's not
a place you spend a lot of time.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:42.516
And as I ran through, I saw this
familiar student, I said, hey Caroline,

00:06:42.516 --> 00:06:43.204
how are you?

00:06:43.204 --> 00:06:44.689
And she goes, no I'm not Caroline.

00:06:46.598 --> 00:06:49.000
I'm sorry, aren't you in my CAT class?

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.364
She goes, that's my twin sister.

00:06:52.364 --> 00:06:56.688
I didn't know that we had both Caroline
and Catha in last year's first-year class,

00:06:56.688 --> 00:06:58.500
they're now rising second years.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.860
And the first time I got to know Monica
was when Monica sent me an email,

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.920
last March and she said, Professor Shrom,
I've not had you in any classes yet.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.228
But two friends, Caroline, Catha and
I have started our own venture and

00:07:15.228 --> 00:07:19.753
we have a real opportunity to talk
about our venture at the Ted X

00:07:19.753 --> 00:07:24.819
conference that Stanford's hosting in May,
but we need some help.

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.200
Would you meet us and coach us?

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:32.487
And that began my relationship
with Caroline, Catha, and Monica,

00:07:32.487 --> 00:07:35.867
who have founded an organization
called Farm to Cup.

00:07:35.867 --> 00:07:41.777
And their goal is to eliminate
the intermediaries and

00:07:41.777 --> 00:07:47.029
get rich,
rich wonderful coffee directly from

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:52.806
Guatemalan farms into your
cup in a very efficient,

00:07:52.806 --> 00:07:56.502
effective, and inspiring way.

00:07:56.502 --> 00:07:59.518
Now I worked with the three of them for
several different sessions, and

00:07:59.518 --> 00:08:04.177
in those sessions, we Figured out
the story that they were going to tell.

00:08:04.177 --> 00:08:08.741
We figured out what details to include and
not, how many of them should speak when

00:08:08.741 --> 00:08:12.435
they did their short three-minute
speech at the TEDx event, and

00:08:12.435 --> 00:08:15.040
had them all ready for their audition.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.800
They did their video, and
they sent their video off to TEDx so

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.420
that they could be a part of
the TEDx event here at Stanford.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:23.980
And they were turned down.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.640
They weren't chosen to be a part of that.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:28.690
And I was disappointed.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:30.330
They were extremely disappointed.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:33.850
They did get to present their coffee and
serve their coffee, but

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:37.730
they didn't get a moment on
the stage to talk about farm to cup.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:44.940
But in August, I got an email from Caffa,
letting me know that the next morning and

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.480
I should turn on NBC News early in the day

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.750
because they were talking about
on the morning news show.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:55.570
Using the coaching that I had
given them to get ready for

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.270
the event that they never got to speak at.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:04.010
Right now, as we speak,
the three of them are in Guatemala,

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:07.790
putting together more relationships so
that they can show up next week, start

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.600
their second year as MBA students and
continue pursuing their business venture.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.250
And they're just one of many examples of

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:21.320
the privilege that I get to work
with MBA students here at the GSB.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:24.410
So when Amanda said, would you come in and

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.130
work with Alumni, to help them tell
their stories more effectively?

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:30.660
It took me about a quarter
of a second to say you bet.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.800
I would love to have
that chance to do that.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:39.076
So what we're going to do tonight,
is not presentation skills 101.

00:09:39.076 --> 00:09:41.664
First of all,
as I listen to the introductions,

00:09:41.664 --> 00:09:43.442
I don't think it's necessary.

00:09:43.442 --> 00:09:46.942
And secondly, if that is something
you have a need or concern for,

00:09:46.942 --> 00:09:49.580
I've got some resources
that I will point you do.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.682
We're going to actually
do the advanced level.

00:09:51.682 --> 00:09:55.830
We're going to do the second year elective
instead of the first year required class.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.308
And we're going to look
specifically at story telling and

00:09:59.308 --> 00:10:02.160
it's a concise storytelling.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:07.810
How do I tell the story of my cause,
my venture, my start up, in a very

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:11.890
short amount of time that causes an impact
for the audience that I'm trying to serve?

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:17.050
The way I'm going to do this is I'm
going to talk for a little bit.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:22.740
I've come up with no real
apologies to Stephen Covey.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.064
Seven habits of highly
concise storytellers, and

00:10:26.064 --> 00:10:28.546
I will give you what those habits are.

00:10:28.546 --> 00:10:33.411
You'll also quickly know that I love
teaching with video, and I particularly

00:10:33.411 --> 00:10:38.070
think all of the video clips I plan
to share tonight, are from Ted.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.044
Is anybody unfamiliar with Ted?

00:10:40.044 --> 00:10:41.130
Anybody not know?

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.250
Alice is not familiar with Ted,
okay, couple.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:46.805
Ted is an amazing website, I'm going to
give you a handout in a minute that

00:10:46.805 --> 00:10:48.478
gives you a way to start on there.

00:10:48.478 --> 00:10:53.250
Ted.com is a great website for
seen presentations.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.097
None of them are more than 18 minutes long
but what people don't know is that they

00:10:57.097 --> 00:11:00.960
have a special section on their website
that's presentations under 6 minutes.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.171
And I'm going to use
some of those clips so

00:11:03.171 --> 00:11:07.970
that you can really see some examples
of concise powerful storytellers.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.540
TED is a conference, it stands for
Technology, Entertainment, and

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:16.963
Design, and I love to just entertain
myself by watching TED Talks.

00:11:16.963 --> 00:11:20.500
My husband wanted me to clean the garage
yesterday because we were having friends

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.290
over last night.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:23.372
[LAUGH] I was like cleaning the garage.

00:11:23.372 --> 00:11:25.490
[LAUGH] That's the job I get.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.640
So I took my laptop down to the garage and
I turned on ted.com and

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.500
I was putting stuff away and
moving the bikes and sweeping but

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:37.780
I had TED Talks going in the background,
so I'm a geek, but I'm a fan of Ted.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:39.620
And I will before you leave.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.670
I'm going to not use any slides tonight.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:42.924
I'm going to write
a little bit on the board.

00:11:42.924 --> 00:11:44.847
We're going to have a conversation, but

00:11:44.847 --> 00:11:48.692
I will give you one handout that has some
links from TED and some links from other

00:11:48.692 --> 00:11:52.820
places hereat the GSP that I hope will
help you in preparing your story.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:58.029
Also have 1, possibly 2, volunteers who
are far enough along in their prep for

00:11:58.029 --> 00:12:00.680
the 40th anniversary event.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.560
Then, in the second half of our time
together there actually going to

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:06.660
come up and do part of their story and
I’m going to coach them in front of you.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:11.040
And we'll see what we can do to make
their stories more concise and stronger.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:15.521
And then, we'll also figure out a way
that we're offering coaching for

00:12:15.521 --> 00:12:18.987
alumni as they prepare for
the event on October 20th.

00:12:18.987 --> 00:12:19.520
Is it the 20th?

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:24.330
And we'll figure how to make sure that
you know that that's available to you.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:27.890
We are taping tonight because some
people weren't able to be here.

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:31.903
So if you do speak up, feel free but know
that you will be captured on audio and

00:12:31.903 --> 00:12:35.418
that it will be made available to
other alumni who are preparing for

00:12:35.418 --> 00:12:37.766
the same thing that you're preparing for.

00:12:37.766 --> 00:12:41.330
So with that said,
let's move in to the conversation.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:47.530
And it's interesting that we're
looking at concise storytelling.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:51.930
Friday afternoon, I got to spend two or
three hours at Oracle, and

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:56.982
I was invited there by,
they have somebody whose job title is

00:12:56.982 --> 00:13:04.250
head writer for
corporate cinema at Oracle.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:06.025
Like, how cool of a job is that?

00:13:06.025 --> 00:13:10.372
I hear head writer, and I think of
the Alan Brady show and Dick Van Dike, and

00:13:10.372 --> 00:13:14.513
what he does is he writes all of
the scripts for all of the corporate video

00:13:14.513 --> 00:13:19.160
that Oracle does to communicate to their
clients and users about the products.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.139
Nothing more than two minutes long.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.431
Highly technical,
a great deal of technical information, but

00:13:29.431 --> 00:13:31.377
all in two minutes or less.

00:13:31.377 --> 00:13:34.679
Now, he will do a series,
kind of like a serial,

00:13:34.679 --> 00:13:38.200
where there will be different parts to it.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:41.170
But they don't put a 20
minute video up online.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.610
They'll put ten two minute videos up and

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:47.990
they actually can track their
effectiveness by how people click through.

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:52.680
And so, the whole idea as
attention spans get shorter,

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:57.830
as competing things on our computer
are coming at us, shorter is better.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:03.139
And so, looking at concision I think is
a good place I think for us to start.

00:14:03.139 --> 00:14:06.160
Let's start with one example from TED.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:10.910
For people who are watching on the video,
we will also be providing

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:15.370
