00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.210
We watched a video last week together.

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:16.140
I think we watched it together.

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:17.160
The Congressman?

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:18.530
Do you remember his final words?

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:25.000
Two words, get right.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:29.757
He did a short TED talk about how to
write to your elected officials and

00:29:29.757 --> 00:29:31.653
he ended it, get writing.

00:29:31.653 --> 00:29:33.390
So think about the last words.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.169
What were my first words tonight?

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:39.990
I first met.

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:40.540
&gt;&gt; Say it again, Diego.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:41.710
&gt;&gt; I first met.

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:46.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I first met Caroline in my CAP course.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:50.460
I could have begun with a lot of
things but in those seven words,

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:53.760
I wanted to give to you
a lot of information.

00:29:54.970 --> 00:29:58.700
I wanted to bring into effect
my relationship with students,

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.980
I wanted to talk about
a current first year student,

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:04.540
I wanted to give you her name so
there's a little bit of a detail there and

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:07.850
I wanted to tie it into a piece of
the curriculum here at the GSP.

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:12.306
So I had a lot that was going
on just in those few words.

00:30:12.306 --> 00:30:16.360
As you look at Mark Betso's clip.

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:17.520
I have something from [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:19.600
We're going to come to
that one in just a minute.

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:20.838
What were his first words?

00:30:20.838 --> 00:30:22.213
Do you remember?

00:30:24.235 --> 00:30:25.888
&gt;&gt; Fire them.

00:30:25.888 --> 00:30:27.974
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Close.

00:30:27.974 --> 00:30:30.910
I actually may have to
watch it again to see.

00:30:30.910 --> 00:30:35.680
But I think it was as a volunteer fireman,
you want to get there first.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:38.040
It was something along those lines.

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:40.387
because he was talking about
the race between him and

00:30:40.387 --> 00:30:42.801
the other guy to get
the assignment there first.

00:30:42.801 --> 00:30:45.680
I've not watched his clip
enough to have it memorized.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:48.000
But think about your own first words.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:52.555
What are the worst first words that
you can use in a presentation?

00:30:52.555 --> 00:30:55.007
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] My name's JD.

00:30:55.007 --> 00:30:58.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Particularly if you're
name's not JD, really bad choice.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:30:59.420
That.
&gt;&gt; What I'd like to talk to you about.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:03.130
&gt;&gt; Hi I'm here to talk to you about.

00:31:03.130 --> 00:31:09.450
Hi my name is JD I'm here today because,
and what happens is particularly in

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:15.120
professional settings there's a slide
that says JD Schrum behind you, there's

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:18.909
an introducer who says today we have with
us JD Schrum and then I get up and say.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:21.728
Hi my name is JD Schrum.

00:31:21.728 --> 00:31:24.727
Use those first words valuable and
think about and

00:31:24.727 --> 00:31:29.300
maybe you want to say them multiple times,
maybe they become like a mantra or

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:33.920
a chorus inside of your presentation,
but think them through.

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:38.630
With my students that I lead during
orientations and introductions to,

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:40.724
I often begin with this phrase.

00:31:40.724 --> 00:31:45.098
I believe you can achieve
mastery at anything you set your

00:31:45.098 --> 00:31:49.578
mind to in the next two years
while you're here at the GSB.

00:31:49.578 --> 00:31:53.205
And then I go into defining what mastery
means, what two years looks like,

00:31:53.205 --> 00:31:54.600
and how they can achieve it.

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:56.050
So thinking through, first words.

00:31:58.100 --> 00:31:59.076
Question.
&gt;&gt; Yes, please.

00:31:59.076 --> 00:32:02.142
And is it Val or Belle?

00:32:02.142 --> 00:32:05.454
&gt;&gt; Val or Valerie or-
&gt;&gt; Val, okay, I had a 50/50 chance.

00:32:05.454 --> 00:32:08.098
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, Val.

00:32:08.098 --> 00:32:09.924
&gt;&gt; So will we be introduced so

00:32:09.924 --> 00:32:13.992
we don't need to introduce
ourself in this four minutes?

00:32:13.992 --> 00:32:14.677
&gt;&gt; It says to be determined.

00:32:14.677 --> 00:32:15.710
I will let you know.

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:16.468
I'm not sure exactly.

00:32:16.468 --> 00:32:20.364
&gt;&gt; Okay, because if nobody introduces us,
I'm going to say, I'm Val Carpenter,

00:32:20.364 --> 00:32:23.458
mayor pro tem the city of Los Altos,
and then go into my speech.

00:32:23.458 --> 00:32:25.788
&gt;&gt; So
you might think about it a little bit.

00:32:25.788 --> 00:32:29.498
You could actually begin with
a little bit of the story.

00:32:29.498 --> 00:32:35.980
A voter came to me, and she was so
incredibly upset because of da, da, da.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.630
I'm Val Carpenter,
I'm the mayor of, and this is what.

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:41.230
So you may be able to
just flip it barely and

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:44.420
still start with something
that catches them off guard.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:47.740
And we'll find out logistically
what it looks like.

00:32:47.740 --> 00:32:51.096
The other piece,
when you're in other settings,

00:32:51.096 --> 00:32:54.773
we often print the whole bio
of somebody in the program.

00:32:54.773 --> 00:32:57.997
Or it's sent out in the invitation,
it's available on the website, and

00:32:57.997 --> 00:33:01.067
then the person who does the introduction
just reads what we already.

00:33:01.067 --> 00:33:03.320
What a waste of an opportunity.

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:06.730
If you're doing an introduction
somewhere other than in the PnP thing,

00:33:06.730 --> 00:33:09.080
find out something that's
not in the printed bio.

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:13.270
Find a story, find an anecdote, find
something that lets you actually serve

00:33:13.270 --> 00:33:17.531
the purpose, not just read what we read
while we were waiting for you to begin.

00:33:17.531 --> 00:33:24.615
Okay, third one, follow Goldilocks.

00:33:28.935 --> 00:33:34.200
Anybody with children remember, anybody
remember what the Goldilocks theory is?

00:33:36.266 --> 00:33:38.150
Jenna, you gotta have a shot at this.

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:39.540
You've got three of them at home.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.810
&gt;&gt; Try a lot of different things
til you find the one that fits?

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:45.680
&gt;&gt; Close, very close.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:49.674
Because every part of the Goldilocks
story, there's something that's too hard,

00:33:49.674 --> 00:33:53.513
there's something that's too soft,
there's something that's just right.

00:33:53.513 --> 00:33:56.444
Something that's too big, something that's
too small, something that's just right.

00:33:56.444 --> 00:33:59.130
When we look at details in our story,

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:02.606
we want to have just
the right amount of details.

00:34:02.606 --> 00:34:08.050
Had Mark gone on and on and
on about the color of the truck and

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:13.620
the address and the street,
it would have ruined it for us.

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:16.400
And where we felt there was
just a little bit of a gap,

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:18.090
we would've liked maybe a detail or
two more.

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:24.060
And that's really a nuance that
you have to, how much do I share,

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:26.803
how little do I share in
terms of the details?

00:34:26.803 --> 00:34:32.540
And thinking through, what are enough
details that it's illustrative,

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:35.360
but not so many that I bored people?

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:40.395
The fourth one, and
I thought Mark did this very well,

00:34:40.395 --> 00:34:43.801
practice one person, one thought.

00:34:46.325 --> 00:34:49.564
And particularly on the day
of the event in October,

00:34:49.564 --> 00:34:54.208
there'll be a camera in the room,
like there is right now in the room here.

00:34:54.208 --> 00:34:56.501
But you're not speaking to the camera,

00:34:56.501 --> 00:34:59.410
you're having a conversation
with individuals.

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:04.501
And what you want to be able to do
is deliver one complete thought to

00:35:04.501 --> 00:35:06.853
one person, kind of have that moment.

00:35:06.853 --> 00:35:09.893
And then deliver another complete
thought to another person,

00:35:09.893 --> 00:35:13.690
actually have that moment, and
then another thought somewhere else.

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:18.250
There's a place, and if you watch
Mark's video again on your own at home,

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:21.520
there's a place where he's
talking about different

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:25.120
things that you can do
that are small steps.

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:27.298
And he delivers one to one
third of the audience,

00:35:27.298 --> 00:35:30.719
one to another third of the audience,
one to another third of the audience.

00:35:30.719 --> 00:35:34.231
You can tell he's connecting
with sections of the room,

00:35:34.231 --> 00:35:37.070
not just scattering across everywhere.

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:39.675
Or not just having one
conversation with one person and

00:35:39.675 --> 00:35:43.705
leaving everybody else out in the loop,
but actually practicing that eye contact.

00:35:43.705 --> 00:35:48.100
Eye contact is gold in storytelling.

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:52.670
And the more that you're actually able to
connect and pierce into somebody's eyes,

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:55.208
the more that you bring
down that resistance and

00:35:55.208 --> 00:35:57.317
the more you can make that connection.

00:35:57.317 --> 00:36:00.543
The fifth one,
this'll take a little bit of,

00:36:00.543 --> 00:36:03.370
this'll take a little bit of work here.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:08.778
Remember the magic grain truck.

00:36:08.778 --> 00:36:14.723
So I grew up in Kansas,
and my high school English

00:36:14.723 --> 00:36:21.258
teacher David Westling was
one of my best educators.

00:36:21.258 --> 00:36:24.531
And he used to tell us, now,
I grew up in Hayes, Kansas, and

00:36:24.531 --> 00:36:28.930
all the kids in my high school freshman
English class were primarily farm boys.

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:35.370
I was not, I was a city kid,
if you can call a city 13,000.

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:38.240
But we all knew what a grain truck was.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:41.830
When we harvested in June,
the grain truck came, and

00:36:41.830 --> 00:36:46.580
they dumped all of the wheat out of
the truck into the grain elevator.

00:36:46.580 --> 00:36:48.194
And that was what the farmer got paid.

00:36:48.194 --> 00:36:49.853
How much wheat was in the truck, and

00:36:49.853 --> 00:36:52.690
what was the water content of
the wheat that was in the truck?

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:55.527
How valuable was the wheat
that was in the truck?

00:36:55.527 --> 00:37:00.440
Mr. Westling said,
poetry is like a magic wheat truck.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:05.961
If there was a way to get
12 truckloads of wheat

00:37:05.961 --> 00:37:10.938
in one truck, how valuable would that be?

00:37:10.938 --> 00:37:12.443
We're like, wow,
that would be pretty amazing.

00:37:12.443 --> 00:37:15.017
You'd only make 1 trip,
instead of 12, from the farm,

00:37:15.017 --> 00:37:16.615
and you'd make all that much money.

00:37:16.615 --> 00:37:19.760
He said, that's what poetry is.

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:24.544
It's the chance to put that much
more weight into a few number of

00:37:24.544 --> 00:37:28.075
words that they carry
that much more meaning.

00:37:28.075 --> 00:37:31.160
Now, I'm not asking that
you become Shakespeare.

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:36.495
But think through,
is there word choices you can

00:37:36.495 --> 00:37:42.885
use that carry more of a impact
then a whole bunch of words?

00:37:42.885 --> 00:37:47.713
For example, in Mark Bezos' talk,
what did he call the other fireman?

00:37:47.713 --> 00:37:48.811
&gt;&gt; Lex Luthor.

00:37:48.811 --> 00:37:51.260
&gt;&gt; Lex Luthor, two words.

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:56.102
What does all of that conjure
up when he says Lex Luthor?

00:37:56.102 --> 00:37:56.849
&gt;&gt; The bad guy.

00:37:56.849 --> 00:38:01.860
&gt;&gt; The bad guy, manipulative, one time,
he later used the word, my nemesis, okay?

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:06.904
All of that comes through by
calling the fireman Lex Luthor.

00:38:06.904 --> 00:38:10.660
&gt;&gt; But funny and cartoonish,
not calling him Hitler or somebody.

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:12.790
&gt;&gt; Exactly, exactly,
that would have only been one word.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:16.526
But that would have, man, a whole
different characterization out of that.

00:38:16.526 --> 00:38:17.830
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] Look like Lex Luthor.

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:18.774
&gt;&gt; A little bit, he did.

00:38:18.774 --> 00:38:19.620
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And so you want to be,
and you also have to be careful.

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:26.086
Like in some audiences,
Lex Luthor would mean nothing.

00:38:26.086 --> 00:38:30.426
I was teaching undergrads last year, and I
was trying to explain the term, I think I

00:38:30.426 --> 00:38:34.650
told this to the coaches last week, I was
trying to explain the term philistine.

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:36.640
Somebody was being a real
philistine about something.

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:39.150
I said, like Archie Bunker.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.586
No clue who I was talking about,
none whatsoever.

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:45.799
And I said, like Homer Simpson.

00:38:45.799 --> 00:38:47.246
Yeah, now we know what
you're talking about.

00:38:47.246 --> 00:38:50.007
So you have to be careful about that,
but think about,

00:38:50.007 --> 00:38:53.250
is there some poetry I can use,
is there some imagery?

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:57.140
Or maybe there's an analogy,
maybe there is a visual image.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:39:01.140
We talked earlier, Jack, about,
you've got some visual images in your.

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:02.689
Sometimes a picture is worth 1,000 words.

00:39:02.689 --> 00:39:06.800
I can put one thing up, and
it conveys a great deal of meaning.

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:11.680
So think about what you can do in your
four minutes that lets you get more out of

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:16.775
a word or more out of a slide
than just one word or one image.

00:39:20.038 --> 00:39:20.870
Anybody a musician in here?

00:39:23.831 --> 00:39:24.543
Yuri, is it?

00:39:24.543 --> 00:39:25.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Am I pronouncing that right?

00:39:25.755 --> 00:39:26.413
What do you play?

00:39:26.413 --> 00:39:30.616
&gt;&gt; Guitar, French horn, saxophone.

00:39:30.616 --> 00:39:31.370
&gt;&gt; Kind of a slacker?

00:39:31.370 --> 00:39:32.659
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:32.659 --> 00:39:33.298
&gt;&gt; Do you read sheet music?

00:39:33.298 --> 00:39:34.566
&gt;&gt; I do on saxophone.

00:39:34.566 --> 00:39:41.350
&gt;&gt; Okay, and
what is a rest in sheet music?

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:43.345
What does that indicate?

00:39:43.345 --> 00:39:43.934
&gt;&gt; A pause in the music.
So

00:39:43.934 --> 00:39:47.636
for a certain amount of time, basically,
you measure it out and you stop playing.

00:39:47.636 --> 00:39:55.100
&gt;&gt; And when somebody composes a piece
of music, they decide in that moment,

00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:01.573
Urie should stop playing the saxophone.

00:40:01.573 --> 00:40:06.586
The silence is going to actually
be written into the composition.

00:40:06.586 --> 00:40:12.627
It's especially powerful when you
see an entire symphony go silent for

00:40:12.627 --> 00:40:17.661
a moment, because the composer
has decided that is the time

00:40:17.661 --> 00:40:22.592
where the music is that's
represented by no notes at all,

00:40:22.592 --> 00:40:27.148
and so for us,
the sixth strategy is too use silence.

00:40:35.941 --> 00:40:39.724
We can use silence to let
people catch up to us,

00:40:39.724 --> 00:40:45.835
we can use silence to frame something,
like the first time we use a phrase,

00:40:45.835 --> 00:40:49.230
or an acronym, we may say here at the GSP.

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:51.470
I may need to frame it a little bit.

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:56.765
Or I can just use it, because it allows
me to get everyone's attention again.

00:40:56.765 --> 00:41:01.546
Those who are my age, or older may
remember an old TV commercial where

00:41:01.546 --> 00:41:06.089
they said, if you want to get
somebody's attention, whisper.

00:41:06.089 --> 00:41:11.470
Want to get somebody's attention in
a class, or in a presentation, be silent.

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:14.877
Why did they stop talking?

00:41:14.877 --> 00:41:15.429
What's going on?

00:41:15.429 --> 00:41:16.979
What do I need to pay attention to now?

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:21.656
And just a few seconds of silence,
appropriately used,

00:41:21.656 --> 00:41:24.241
can add emphasis to your presentation.

00:41:26.285 --> 00:41:31.099
And the seventh strategy that I'm

00:41:31.099 --> 00:41:35.747
going to ask you to do is know your AIM

00:41:35.747 --> 00:41:40.452
that's an acronym that we use.

00:41:40.452 --> 00:41:42.959
Actually, I've got enough
people here that maybe.

00:41:42.959 --> 00:41:46.570
Anybody have Mary Munter when
you were here at school?

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:49.310
So, this is something
Mary Munter came up with.

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:51.530
Mary Munter taught
communication here at the GSP.

00:41:51.530 --> 00:41:56.300
She'd been to the top school at Dartmouth,
and she is one of my mentors, and

00:41:56.300 --> 00:41:57.950
I use several of her
books in my teachings.

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:03.136
And it stands for
audience intent and message.

00:42:03.136 --> 00:42:05.020
Who's my audience?

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:08.190
What do I want them to do as
a result of this communication,

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:10.330
what's my intent, what's my objective?

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:12.475
And then how do I frame
my message in that way.

00:42:12.475 --> 00:42:17.090
Getting back to Diego's question, you got
a lot of complicated information about.

00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:20.210
That you want to be able to share about
your entity, about your enterprise,

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:21.850
about your start up.

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:26.330
With a certain audience,
you may share this information.

00:42:26.330 --> 00:42:30.412
With investors, with partners,
it may be this information.

00:42:30.412 --> 00:42:33.726
So, we decide based on who
our audience is what we want

00:42:33.726 --> 00:42:37.990
them to do with that information,
how much we share.

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:42.374
The best thing to happen, now this isn't
a part of the PMP formats of fame,

00:42:42.374 --> 00:42:47.459
the best thing to happen, especially for
entrepreneurs, and non-profit leaders,

00:42:47.459 --> 00:42:51.288
and people who are involved in
causes is to share a little bit, and

00:42:51.288 --> 00:42:52.914
have them ask to hear more.

00:42:52.914 --> 00:42:56.210
And share a little bit and
have them ask to hear more.

00:42:56.210 --> 00:42:59.390
You've been at one of those cocktail
parties here in Silicon Valley

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:03.510
where somebody says, let me tell you about
my start up, and they launch onto you, and

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:07.920
they just keep going, you're trying to get
to the bar, trying to get away from them?

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:09.745
As opposed to hooking them and

00:43:09.745 --> 00:43:13.835
saying enough that they want to
engage in a conversation with you.

00:43:13.835 --> 00:43:17.851
So, knowing your audience, and what do
you want them to do with the information.

00:43:17.851 --> 00:43:22.558
Now, can we take a moment here, and
is this appropriate to talk about

00:43:22.558 --> 00:43:27.260
the two possible branches that
their presentations make away?

00:43:27.260 --> 00:43:29.068
Can you explain that better than I can?

00:43:29.068 --> 00:43:33.316
&gt;&gt; Sure, so I think,
where all of you volunteer to be speakers,

00:43:33.316 --> 00:43:38.630
at the time we were aiming for 18 people,
and imagine we would have everyone

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:44.215
in the London room with a consolidated
audience, maybe about 100, 150.

00:43:44.215 --> 00:43:47.873
We weren't really sure what to expect,
because this is the first time in the day

00:43:47.873 --> 00:43:50.620
where we pulled together
the fellow my community this way.

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:54.282
Since then we have had almost 40
people come forward with interest, and

00:43:54.282 --> 00:43:58.239
we actually had to shut off the accepting
of new people that you know in the spirit

00:43:58.239 --> 00:44:02.045
of really wanting to represent alumni
we haven't heard from in a long time.

00:44:02.045 --> 00:44:04.403
Not just those who are really
piped into the JC community.

00:44:04.403 --> 00:44:07.715
And folks who have a variety of stories,
we're going to try and

00:44:07.715 --> 00:44:11.170
accommodate about 40 people,
and do two simultaneous rooms.

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:18.058
We're figuring out exactly how it's going,
maybe where we all start together,

00:44:18.058 --> 00:44:20.662
listen to a number of introductory talks,
and

00:44:20.662 --> 00:44:24.031
then there becomes a side room
where others are available.

00:44:24.031 --> 00:44:25.506
They may both be kind of 50,

00:44:25.506 --> 00:44:29.637
50 splits, but the theme that seems to be
emerging is that some of you did come into

00:44:29.637 --> 00:44:33.433
this wanting to talk about the
organization, and I originally had posted.

00:44:33.433 --> 00:44:35.370
To please pick out a pitch.

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:37.320
Make this more so about yourself.

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:39.067
And it seems that there's
kind of a natural interest

00:44:39.067 --> 00:44:41.101
of some people to make a little
bit more of an explicit pitch.

00:44:41.101 --> 00:44:46.250
Whether it be for investors, or
just action, or engagement, or volunteers.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:49.812
And so, that's what we're looking at
is that there may be actually, for

00:44:49.812 --> 00:44:53.432
some of you who want to make a little bit
more of an explicit ask of some form,

00:44:53.432 --> 00:44:56.534
a place to do that, whereas others
might have a little bit more of

00:44:56.534 --> 00:44:58.393
a kind of reflective story of impact.

00:44:58.393 --> 00:45:00.890
So, regardless,
these tools are useful to you.

00:45:00.890 --> 00:45:03.670
And this is something that as we're
finalizing the list of speakers,

00:45:03.670 --> 00:45:05.220
I'll be getting back to you about.

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:09.586
If, as you hear this, you feel strongly
that you belong in one place, or

00:45:09.586 --> 00:45:14.039
the other, and you don't think it's
self evident, please let me know.

00:45:14.039 --> 00:45:16.581
But, if you have questions,
I'm happy to answer them.

00:45:16.581 --> 00:45:17.552
&gt;&gt; I have a question.

00:45:17.552 --> 00:45:21.108
So, will you announce what these
two categories of rooms are so

00:45:21.108 --> 00:45:24.206
the audience can choose which
group they want to go to?

00:45:24.206 --> 00:45:28.064
&gt;&gt; Yes, and there will be signage with
the program of the topic, saying,

00:45:28.064 --> 00:45:29.910
this is the schedule.

00:45:29.910 --> 00:45:32.780
Say, three to five what the schedule for
class outside,

00:45:32.780 --> 00:45:35.750
neither of two room options for
that period of time.

00:45:35.750 --> 00:45:39.480
And it kind of guides what's in
that space by the amount of area,

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:42.900
or if it ends up being this pitch,
maybe we all start in one space.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:44.900
And then there is a reception,
or a break, and

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:49.727
those who want to hear the more explicit
pitches are rushed to another space.

00:45:49.727 --> 00:45:52.540
And it would either be in the program,
or announced.

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:57.640
In advance and then we sign and share, and
that helps everybody know where we went.

00:45:57.640 --> 00:46:00.700
&gt;&gt; Just a little confused
about what to talk about.

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:04.200
I don't want to pitch anything,
but I also, I mean,

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:07.770
it was the original point to just show
different perspectives of folks who

00:46:07.770 --> 00:46:12.290
went through the P&amp;P, and
how they now are reflecting.

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:16.347
Years later on their various interactions
with the public environment, or-

00:46:16.347 --> 00:46:17.530
&gt;&gt; You know,

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:21.370
in your case, we talked a little
bit about you having this kind of

00:46:21.370 --> 00:46:26.180
large kind of view from the top of social
innovation as this emerging theme, and

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:27.850
kind of the feel of
social entrepreneurship.

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:29.990
So, I still think it makes sense for
you and

00:46:29.990 --> 00:46:32.940
Ann to probably to kind
of share your wisdom.

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:36.418
In more of the interact pitch, but

00:46:36.418 --> 00:46:41.100
we can talk about that a little bit
more as you two talk to each other.

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:44.673
She's a colleague who is also at UCLM
that will be talking as well and

00:46:44.673 --> 00:46:46.123
kind of pair that together.

00:46:46.123 --> 00:46:49.320
So, we can explore that and
see what feels the most exciting to you.

00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:49.998
But I think,

00:46:49.998 --> 00:46:54.020
that working with a fellow alumni
would be a neat facet to your story.

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:58.557
And I'm the fact that you engaged
other students in the work of your

00:46:58.557 --> 00:47:02.010
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:07.560
as we look at the intent, whether you're
in the first room, or the second room,

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:11.520
or you find yourself in another setting
where you're talking about the things that

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:16.790
you're up to, it's that measurement
of by sharing this information,

00:47:16.790 --> 00:47:19.140
what do I want the audience to do with it?

00:47:19.140 --> 00:47:24.030
And sometimes, it's very explicit, that I
want to engage people as volunteers into

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:26.910
my organization, or
as donors into my organization.

00:47:26.910 --> 00:47:31.980
Sometimes, it's more that I
want them to embrace the idea,

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:34.940
or I want them to continue
the conversation, or

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:39.040
I want them to reach out to somebody else,
and re-share this information.

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:42.800
So, it's thinking that through, what do
I want people to do as a result of it?

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:48.159
We had Nancy Duarte speak here at the GSB
last year, and she talked about that

00:47:48.159 --> 00:47:53.886
she believes in business there is no such
thing as an informative presentation.

00:47:53.886 --> 00:47:55.820
Everything at some level is persuasive.

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:59.060
Me spending these two hours with you,

00:47:59.060 --> 00:48:02.005
I want you to deploy these tactics
when you deliver your presentation.

00:48:02.005 --> 00:48:05.180
I'm not just giving you information,
I want you to practice it.

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:06.248
I want you to do it.

00:48:06.248 --> 00:48:08.274
And so thinking through,

00:48:08.274 --> 00:48:13.530
what is it that I can get people to
do as a result of this information?

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:18.270
So those are my seven habits of
highly concise storytellers,

00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:22.680
but I thought a lot about
the experience of being alumni.

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:26.152
And I think it's great for you to be able
to come back to the GSB and hear from

00:48:26.152 --> 00:48:29.825
a faculty member, a distinguished and
and well-regarded faculty member.

00:48:29.825 --> 00:48:32.362
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; None of them were available tonight and

00:48:32.362 --> 00:48:34.460
so I was able to come.

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:37.290
But I thought what was actually
more useful would be for

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:38.769
you to be able to hear from students.

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:43.550
Because often when you're back,
you may get to interact with students.

00:48:43.550 --> 00:48:45.500
But it's rare that you
get to go to a class.

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:49.855
It's rare that you actually get more than
kind of networking sorts of conversations

00:48:49.855 --> 00:48:50.911
with our students.

00:48:50.911 --> 00:48:55.853
So I've brought some of our students
into the workshop this evening

00:48:55.853 --> 00:48:59.960
to talk to you about their
experience in storytelling.

00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:05.548
And what will happen is you will
see some of my students on,

00:49:05.548 --> 00:49:11.610
let's see, I need the screen down,
that's the lower arrow.

00:49:11.610 --> 00:49:16.554
In the class that I teach here at the GSB
called Strategic Communication, and

00:49:16.554 --> 00:49:20.587
actually Mary is starting to
teach Strategic Communication,

00:49:20.587 --> 00:49:24.340
next Monday is the first
class with her students.

00:49:24.340 --> 00:49:29.835
We do a final project that students
do a 10 to 15 minute presentation

00:49:29.835 --> 00:49:35.090
to their peers,
about a specific communications topic.

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:41.080
And they deliver it in class, but
then we take the best of those lessons and

00:49:41.080 --> 00:49:43.380
we put them up on the web
as a virtual library for

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:46.860
other students to learn
from on their tactics.

00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:50.074
And I had a group of students two years
ago who did one on effective story

00:49:50.074 --> 00:49:50.577
telling.

00:49:50.577 --> 00:49:54.461
So I'm going to show you just three
minutes of their advice to us on

00:49:54.461 --> 00:49:56.040
storytelling.

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:00.292
You may not be able to see their slides as
well as I wish you could, but when I send

00:50:00.292 --> 00:50:05.600
you the link out, you'll be able to both
watch the video and download their slides.

00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:09.000
So let's see what our GSB students
tell us about storytelling.

00:50:10.910 --> 00:50:12.340
&gt;&gt; To give you a preview
of where we're going,

00:50:12.340 --> 00:50:15.320
we're first going to walk you through
the basics of building a story.

00:50:15.320 --> 00:50:16.270
So what is the story?

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:17.450
What's the structure of a story?

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:19.177
How can it differ?
And what are some tips and

00:50:19.177 --> 00:50:22.685
best practices that you employ next
time you wanted to build a story?

00:50:22.685 --> 00:50:24.330
Next I'll show you an exercise,

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:27.770
where we're all going to take a chance
at writing our own mini stories.

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:30.349
We're then going to give you some
specific tools that you can use,

00:50:30.349 --> 00:50:33.644
some stories that you can put in your back
pocket next time you want to actually pull

00:50:33.644 --> 00:50:34.956
a storytelling presentation.

00:50:34.956 --> 00:50:42.547
&gt;&gt; All right, so, I'm going to talk to you
guys today about how we build a story,

00:50:42.547 --> 00:50:47.121
what do you think about
when you tell a story?

00:50:47.121 --> 00:50:52.691
So, telling a story and giving
a presentation happen to be very similar.

00:50:52.691 --> 00:50:55.940
You're going to have an introduction,
a middle and a conclusion.

00:50:55.940 --> 00:50:59.050
Now there are a few things
that are different however.

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:04.088
In a presentation, you're going to tell
people what you're going to tell them,

00:51:04.088 --> 00:51:06.460
tell them and
then tell them what you just told them.

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:11.610
In a story, you're going to have
three different distinct components,

00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:13.630
and they're each going to
have a different goal.

00:51:14.660 --> 00:51:17.450
So we'll start with your introduction.

00:51:17.450 --> 00:51:20.584
Similar to a presentation, your
introduction is going to be short, and

00:51:20.584 --> 00:51:23.398
you're going to want to drop
audience directly into the action,

00:51:23.398 --> 00:51:24.316
really important.

00:51:24.316 --> 00:51:29.002
There are two other objectives that
you're going to want to have for

00:51:29.002 --> 00:51:30.545
your introduction.

00:51:30.545 --> 00:51:34.670
The first, this is where you
establish background information.

00:51:34.670 --> 00:51:39.626
You give the audience the information
they're going to need in the context for

00:51:39.626 --> 00:51:42.540
understanding where the story's going.

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:45.580
You're also going to establish
the dramatic question.

00:51:45.580 --> 00:51:49.465
The dramatic question is what does
the protagonist want, what is his or

00:51:49.465 --> 00:51:52.790
her goals that they're striving
towards during the story?

00:51:52.790 --> 00:51:54.360
Next is the middle component of the story.

00:51:54.360 --> 00:51:59.320
In the middle component,
you have three other objectives.

00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:03.010
Firstly, you're going to
develop the characters and

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:06.460
the situation that you previously
introduced in the beginning of your story.

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:13.660
Next, you want your major action to
happen in this part of the story.

00:52:13.660 --> 00:52:19.322
And lastly,
you're going to introduce obstacles.

00:52:19.322 --> 00:52:23.710
And you want a bunch of obstacles, and you
want them to be increasingly difficult,

00:52:23.710 --> 00:52:27.720
that the protagonist needs to bump into
again and again trying to get to his or

00:52:27.720 --> 00:52:28.300
her goal.

00:52:28.300 --> 00:52:29.966
Lastly, you're going to have a conclusion.

00:52:29.966 --> 00:52:32.857
Now this conclusion is going to be
the shortest part of your story, but

00:52:32.857 --> 00:52:33.694
the most critical.

00:52:33.694 --> 00:52:37.327
Ideally you want the last act
of the conclusion to give

00:52:37.327 --> 00:52:41.632
the audience the most enjoyment
in the climax in what happens.

00:52:41.632 --> 00:52:46.240
You're going to follow a three
C pattern in your conclusion,

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:50.150
crisis, climax, and consequences.

00:52:50.150 --> 00:52:54.850
The crisis is the point of the most
tension, that's going to happen here.

00:52:54.850 --> 00:52:59.020
The climax is when the crisis point
ends and the tension is released.

00:52:59.020 --> 00:53:02.600
This is where the audience gets
the answer to the dramatic question.

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:05.450
Lastly you’re going to allude
to the consequences that come as

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:06.020
a result of this.

00:53:07.160 --> 00:53:11.656
Now, stories mostly have this template but

00:53:11.656 --> 00:53:16.040
they can have very many different shapes.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:16.540
For instance here's Casablanca.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:19.941
&gt;&gt; The story is a bit wonky but

00:53:19.941 --> 00:53:24.619
always upward sloping to the end.
And

00:53:24.619 --> 00:53:28.840
here's James Bond, boom pow at the
beginning, then it's boring, slowly but

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:30.812
surely he gets the girl.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:53:30.812 --> 00:53:32.213
&gt;&gt; Or you could have Jurassic Park.

00:53:32.213 --> 00:53:37.149
In which, you have a series of crises

00:53:37.149 --> 00:53:41.934
that need to be resolved.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:53:41.934 --> 00:53:44.446
&gt;&gt; All right, so now you have-

00:53:44.446 --> 00:53:47.500
&gt;&gt; Okay, so I liked the way that they used

00:53:47.500 --> 00:53:52.400
movies as a way to look at
the template for stories.

00:53:52.400 --> 00:53:57.410
And the part of the information that
they used in their 12 minute class

00:53:57.410 --> 00:54:02.470
on storytelling is drawn from Jennifer
Acker's class that she teaches here,

00:54:02.470 --> 00:54:05.876
actually right now during September
session on how to tell a story.

00:54:05.876 --> 00:54:09.100
And so some of the tips and
some of the strategies that are in there

00:54:09.100 --> 00:54:13.360
are things that they drew from her class
and then built into ths presentation.

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:16.010
And I will give you the link
that you can watch all of that,

00:54:16.010 --> 00:54:19.280
as well some other student presentations.

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:25.451
But that short one particularly on
storytelling I think is useful,

00:54:25.451 --> 00:54:32.620
but what I liked about it is the sense
of obstacle, climax, and consequence.

00:54:32.620 --> 00:54:36.750
And as you think about your journey,
whether it is as an entrepreneur,

00:54:36.750 --> 00:54:40.260
as an elected official,
as somebody who's starting an entry,

00:54:40.260 --> 00:54:44.800
what are the obstacles
that you have overcome?

00:54:44.800 --> 00:54:46.540
How did you overcome them?

00:54:46.540 --> 00:54:51.035
And where are you at now as a resolution?

00:54:51.035 --> 00:54:53.190
Now, I want you to also vary that.

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:58.100
If all of you go out and do the exact same
format, by the time we get to the end of

00:54:58.100 --> 00:55:01.610
two hours of those presentations
it will be kind of dull.

00:55:01.610 --> 00:55:06.420
There's another resource that I
will encourage you to look at and

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:08.110
some of you have heard me
mention her name earlier,

00:55:08.110 --> 00:55:12.890
Nancy Duarte who's the author
of Resonate and also Slideology.

00:55:12.890 --> 00:55:17.668
She's here in the valley, she comes and
spends a lot time here at the GSB with us,

00:55:17.668 --> 00:55:19.380
working with our students.

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:24.493
She's the woman that did the PowerPoint
slides for Inconvenient Truth.

00:55:24.493 --> 00:55:28.194
So she's the only one who's ever
gotten an Oscar for PowerPoint slides.

00:55:28.194 --> 00:55:34.518
And in Resonate, she talks about that
every presentation follows this,

00:55:34.518 --> 00:55:39.822
sorry folks on the video,
you're not going to get this as well,

00:55:39.822 --> 00:55:46.759
but every story has where things begin,
they get better and then how things are.

00:55:46.759 --> 00:55:51.040
How they could be, how they are,
how they could be, how they are.

00:55:51.040 --> 00:55:53.387
And in the book Resonate,

00:55:53.387 --> 00:55:58.210
she Looks at everything from Martin
Luther King's I Have a Dream Speech.

00:55:58.210 --> 00:56:01.200
This is the way things are,
this is the way they could be,

00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:03.366
this is the way things are,
this is the way they could be.

00:56:03.366 --> 00:56:07.160
[LAUGH] To Steve Jobs'
introduction of the iPhone.

00:56:07.160 --> 00:56:09.663
[LAUGH] This is the way things are,
this is the way they could be,

00:56:09.663 --> 00:56:11.942
this is the way things are,
this is the way they could be.

00:56:11.942 --> 00:56:15.374
And that sense of climax,
that sense of obstacle and

00:56:15.374 --> 00:56:18.940
resolution, is a major
part of storytelling.

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:22.206
And this book in particular,
I think, is wonderful about,

00:56:22.206 --> 00:56:24.779
how do you tell a story
through a presentation?

00:56:24.779 --> 00:56:28.161
Her first book, Slidology, is more about
how do you make the visuals look good,

00:56:28.161 --> 00:56:30.670
this is how do you make sure
the story under it is effective.

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:36.411
We are exactly where I
wanted to be at 8 o'clock.

00:56:36.411 --> 00:56:39.595
Jack, are you ready for
where we wanted to be at 8 o'clock?

00:56:39.595 --> 00:56:40.783
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I guess I am.

00:56:40.783 --> 00:56:44.422
&gt;&gt; Okay,
Jack is far enough along in his prep.

00:56:44.422 --> 00:56:47.983
You want to give me the disk and
I can get it keyed up here?

00:56:47.983 --> 00:56:52.249
Jack is far enough along in his prep that
he said he's willing to do part of his

00:56:52.249 --> 00:56:55.940
presentation and
get a little bit of feedback from me.

00:56:55.940 --> 00:56:58.520
Some coaching in terms of what could
go differently, what's working,

00:56:58.520 --> 00:56:59.990
what could go differently.

00:56:59.990 --> 00:57:02.840
We don't expect that you will
have incorporated everything that

00:57:02.840 --> 00:57:04.250
we have just talked about here.

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:07.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But what this is great to do

00:57:07.580 --> 00:57:11.759
is just to be able to have a dry run and
an audience that's a friendly audience.

00:57:14.360 --> 00:57:16.660
&gt;&gt; So, according to E.O. Wilson,

00:57:16.660 --> 00:57:20.720
today's species are disappearing thousands
of times faster than any time in history.

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:28.575
And this loss is projected to increase
to 10,000 times faster by the year 2050.

00:57:28.575 --> 00:57:32.970
Forest loss is biodiversity loss.

00:57:32.970 --> 00:57:36.610
Humans are responsible for
this, that means you and me.

00:57:38.490 --> 00:57:43.768
We hope our story inspires and
engages you to stop this now.

00:57:50.015 --> 00:57:52.072
Do two things at once.

00:57:52.072 --> 00:57:56.748
Ten years ago, on a visit to
Kangaroo Island, South Australia, Hillary,

00:57:56.748 --> 00:58:01.587
my wife, and I fell in love with the
native black swans and the unique ecology.

00:58:01.587 --> 00:58:05.970
There we found an old neglected sheep and
cattle farm, Signet Park.

00:58:05.970 --> 00:58:10.010
This is an arial of the park of
the farm when we first bought it.

00:58:10.010 --> 00:58:13.840
All of the ground you see was
once a diverse forest ecology

00:58:13.840 --> 00:58:14.730
along a beautiful river.

00:58:16.080 --> 00:58:20.848
All we could imagine was grapes and
olives as far as the eye could see.

00:58:20.848 --> 00:58:27.066
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; While driving the property,

00:58:27.066 --> 00:58:32.640
we found 15 acres of land that was
dead by commercial fertilizer buildup.

00:58:32.640 --> 00:58:34.260
Was there a solution?

00:58:34.260 --> 00:58:38.424
Locals saw this destruction
as unavoidable farming.

00:58:38.424 --> 00:58:41.882
University scientists helped and
three years later,

00:58:41.882 --> 00:58:46.329
the 15 acres had tall grass and
a living ecology was again possible.

00:58:46.329 --> 00:58:49.620
It was our revelation
to species extinction.

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:55.971
So we did the unthinkable to our
friends back in California, and

00:58:55.971 --> 00:59:00.930
expanded our estimation to 800 acres and
fundamentally changed our

00:59:00.930 --> 00:59:06.231
passion from wine and olive oil to
investing in fighting for biodiversity.

00:59:06.231 --> 00:59:10.913
Our land allowed us to uncover the
mysteries of native plant germination and

00:59:10.913 --> 00:59:15.904
cultivation that quickly attracted
hundreds of people who shared our passion.

00:59:15.904 --> 00:59:18.890
No one will miss more bottles of wine and
olive oil.

00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:22.603
But we'll all regret not helping
stop this mass extinction.

00:59:26.141 --> 00:59:28.593
Our efforts spread, word spread, and

00:59:28.593 --> 00:59:33.574
the Australian non-profit Biolar was
born with encouragement of our friend,

00:59:33.574 --> 00:59:38.278
Steve Snyder and his wife of the Wood
Institute, at Stanford, or course.

00:59:38.278 --> 00:59:43.890
With Biolar, we focused on reimagine,
restore, and reinvent biodiversity.

00:59:43.890 --> 00:59:49.170
And new processes for finding and
germinating native seeds.

00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:53.090
Our burning experiments germinated
native seeds long thought extinct.

00:59:54.350 --> 00:59:59.214
More fields were prepared,
experiments expanded, and

00:59:59.214 --> 01:00:03.484
we continue to bio reverse
the extinction trend.

01:00:03.484 --> 01:00:07.160
Surprisingly, we became
the weed control experts.

01:00:07.160 --> 01:00:10.275
Our farm manager made
Signet Park his PhD thesis and

01:00:10.275 --> 01:00:13.836
data gathering efforts exploded
in intensity and scale.

01:00:16.492 --> 01:00:23.242
Annually 450 to 650 volunteers from
six months old to 78 years old,

01:00:23.242 --> 01:00:28.204
from 20 plus countries join
our planting festivals.

01:00:28.204 --> 01:00:31.350
Our first summer fellow was Robin Pam,
a Stanford grad.

01:00:32.540 --> 01:00:35.070
This year we had 100,000 seedlings for

01:00:35.070 --> 01:00:38.740
a total of 750,000
plantings since we started.

01:00:38.740 --> 01:00:44.365
In year one,
our goal was 5,000 trees in our lifetime.

01:00:44.365 --> 01:00:45.557
We scaled further.

01:00:47.912 --> 01:00:51.845
Biodiversity warriors gathered and

01:00:51.845 --> 01:00:56.418
planted, got muddy and dialed some more.

01:00:56.418 --> 01:00:58.350
The trees and understory grew.

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:03.517
We never missed the grapes or olives,
but we celebrate with both every year.

01:01:03.517 --> 01:01:07.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Signet Park is 10 years into

01:01:07.170 --> 01:01:08.750
a 300 year path of biodiversity.

01:01:08.750 --> 01:01:13.920
100 experiments in seed germination,
five plants thought long extinct.

01:01:13.920 --> 01:01:16.580
50 experiments on weed control have

01:01:16.580 --> 01:01:20.750
helped us to continue to reimagine
new ways to slow extinction.

01:01:22.808 --> 01:01:29.933
The glossy black cockatoo is one of
the most endangered birds in the world.

01:01:29.933 --> 01:01:33.391
It eats only one seed,
in one species of tree,

01:01:33.391 --> 01:01:38.380
in one location that is disappearing
rapidly on Kangaroo Island.

01:01:38.380 --> 01:01:42.406
We have doubled the nesting pairs
to eight, fighting back extinction.

01:01:42.406 --> 01:01:46.920
We want Bioar to bio replicate our passion
for partnering with others in Australia

01:01:46.920 --> 01:01:49.590
and the rest of the world
to restore habitats and

01:01:49.590 --> 01:01:52.250
save other animals like the glossy black.

01:01:52.250 --> 01:01:57.590
So get involved in understanding and
reinvesting in biodiversity.

01:01:57.590 --> 01:02:00.890
We've learned that success comes
from small things that at first

01:02:00.890 --> 01:02:01.840
seem unimportant.

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:03.370
And then by thought and

01:02:03.370 --> 01:02:07.520
commitment, become the big things
that really do change the world.

01:02:07.520 --> 01:02:10.133
And in our case, that's one seed and
one volunteer at a time.

